## SECTION 5: AUDIENCE INTELLIGENCE
**who_they_are:** Nail art enthusiasts and TikTok/Pinterest scrollers, mostly Gen Z and younger millennials, who've seen duck/lipstick/flare shapes resurface in their feed and want to understand what they are before booking or trying press-ons. Many are Y2K-nostalgia driven; some are total beginners to acrylic/gel-x shapes.

**what_they_want_from_this_post:** A clear, non-judgmental explainer of what each shape actually is, why it's called that, whether it will look good/last/feel practical on them, and how to try it without full commitment (press-ons vs salon).

**what_frustrates_them:** Existing content is split across three separate single-shape posts (having to piece it together), is either pure gallery-scroll with zero substance or pure how-to-grow-them tutorial with no "should I actually get this" guidance, and rarely covers the Y2K origin story that explains why it feels nostalgic.

**top_5_pain_points:**
1. Confusion over naming — duck vs flare vs fan vs Jersey nails feels like inconsistent terminology for the same or different things
2. Practicality anxiety — driving, typing, catching on clothing/car seats are recurring real concerns from actual wearers
3. Uncertainty whether these shapes will look "good" or "ugly" on their specific hand shape/finger length
4. Not knowing the durability trade-off (wider free edge = less breakage per some sources, but conflicting salon-pro opinions on this)
5. Wanting a low-commitment way to try the look (press-ons) before booking full acrylics

**exact_user_phrases:** "duck bill nails," "why would you want your nails this flared," "duck feet nails," "still not a flare convert but I had a go," "how is one supposed to pick things up," "DUCK NAILS ARE BACK," "lipstick nail shape," "flare/shovel/wide/duck feet nail," "your nails have hips, take em in," "parrot nails"

**intent_content_signals:** Reader responds to clear structural definitions per shape (not vague), real trade-off honesty over hype, before/after or side-by-side visual framing, permission to find the look genuinely divisive/not-for-everyone, low-stakes entry points (press-ons) called out explicitly

